 "note": "[agent-bulk] shw 20014 (211.6 MeV, 7 seg, pdg 11, mcp2k other_em): correct. Vertex is fine and judged from the standard sweep (event half-span 114.6 cm, below the 150 cm re-frame threshold, so no --vtx-span pass was needed): the star sits on the lower end of the 88.1 cm pdg-2212 segment 13001, which is grey/unowned throughout -- the hadron is correctly left out of every shower. 20014 is a detached photon-like object: its reco start is 14.8 cm from the vertex, the member chain runs ~39 cm from there with the axis pointing away from the star, and it holds together as one object under a 140 deg azimuth change. Nothing contiguous is missing: every non-member within 60 cm is at 51-174 deg (13001 at 12.1 cm/143.8, 89021 at 19.5/168.1, 94028 at 38.3/51.7, 96030 at 49.8/80.7, 87019 at 54.1/161.8), i.e. behind or transverse to the axis, and the acceptance plot carries no unclaimed circle at all inside 0-15 deg. The only thing I would call soft is at the other end: 92026 (0.85 cm, pdg 13) at 76.9 cm/4.7 deg and 18005 (2.1 cm, pdg 13) at 103.9 cm/7.6 deg are 1-2 cm stubs absorbed by pass4_angle from 77 and 104 cm out. Both are inside the pass-1 acceptance steps on the acc plot and together they are ~3 cm of track length against the shower's 39, so I did not mark them OUT -- a mark would overstate a negligible over-reach. The finding worth the owner's eye on this event is the pi0, not the clustering: THE RECO MADE NO PI0 GROUP (all pio_id -1) even though the event has two detached EM showers that both back-project to the neutrino vertex -- 20014 (211.6 MeV, start 14.8 cm out) and 99036 (98.5 MeV, 7 seg, 27.2 cm, start 37.4 cm out) -- visibly so in pio-az-055-el+020, where the blue and red gamma axes both point away from the magenta star. I assigned the slots by hand and stored the pairing: Epi0 310.1 MeV, asym 0.36, theta 76.3 deg giving m = 178.3 MeV on the axis convention and theta 68.2 deg giving m = 161.8 MeV on the vertex convention, and the display reports BOTH as inside the code's own accept window. Stored as a candidate, not as a settled pi0: 162-178 MeV is 20-30 pct above 135, so either this is a pi0 with the charge overestimated or it is not a pi0 at all; what I am reporting is the discrepancy between an in-window hand pairing and an empty reco pairing, and the usual caveat that the two mass conventions disagree by 16 MeV on my own pair. Second shower, not the verdict: 99036 is clean in the same way -- its member chain is contiguous over 27 cm with two small on-axis stubs (95029 at 45.7 cm/10.5 deg, 100039 at 56.0 cm/17.2 deg) added by pass4_angle. Third shower, flagged not judged: 91023 (28.4 MeV, 2 seg, 27.4 cm) is a thin straight line lying alongside and parallel to the 88 cm proton 13001 in both the az-055 and az+085 views, i.e. it reads as a track fragment rather than a shower; cluster 91 and cluster 13 may be two pieces of the same hadron. I am not recording a PID verdict on it -- it is not the verdict shower and 28 MeV is too little to settle from these frames. Clustering statement on 20014, silent on PID. Showers NOT looked at: the 9 the scope rule skipped -- 17004 (9.4 MeV), 93027 (4.6), 89021 (3.5), 19006 (1.2), 16003 (1.0), 87019 (0.9), 96030 (0.7), 94028 (0.5), 88020 (0.4) -- all below the 20 MeV threshold.",
